alse,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6302a2f1.gif","alt":"The figure shows the percentage of daily smokers aged ≥18 years, by number of cigarettes smoked per day (CPD), in the United States during 2005-2012. Among daily smokers, declines in mean CPD occurred from 16.7 in 2005 to 14.6 in 2012 (p<0.05 for trend). Increases occurred in the proportion of daily smokers who smoked 1-9 CPD (16.4% to 20.8%) and 10-19 CPD (36.0% to 41.2%), whereas declines occurred in those smoking 20-29 CPD (34.9% to 31.0%) and ≥30 CPD (12.6% to 7.0%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":515,"renderedHeight":369,"naturalWidth":515,"naturalHeight":369,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 475 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":10308},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6302a2f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows quit ratios among ever smokers aged ≥18 years, overall and by age group, in the United States during 2005-2012. Among current smokers and former smokers who quit during the preceding year, 52.9% had made a quit attempt for >1 day. The overall quit ratio (i.e., the ratio of former to ever smokers) increased from 50.7% in 2005 to 55.0% in 2012 (p<0.05). Quit ratios were lowest among adults aged 18-24 years and highest among those aged ≥65 years in each survey year.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":515,"renderedHeight":463,"naturalWidth":515,"naturalHeight":463,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 484 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":12956},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackag